1
00:00:05,867 --> 00:00:08,667
This tiny network has sixteen knobs, called weights.

2
00:00:09,333 --> 00:00:12,467
A real network has thousands, or millions of them.

3
00:00:13,133 --> 00:00:16,133
In code, every knob is just a number.

4
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,333
The loss measures how wrong we are. Lower is better.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,467
Let us turn one knob blindly and watch the loss.

6
00:00:25,133 --> 00:00:29,667
Oops. The mistake got bigger. We turned the wrong way.

7
00:00:30,333 --> 00:00:34,333
Another knob, other direction: lucky, the loss falls.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,800
With thousands of knobs, guessing is hopeless. We need a direction.

9
00:00:40,433 --> 00:00:43,167
Here is our plan for the next chapters.

10
00:00:43,767 --> 00:00:47,167
First we learn about slopes, then about nudges.

11
00:00:47,767 --> 00:00:51,167
Next, the graph and the backward pass.

12
00:00:51,767 --> 00:00:55,167
Then one neuron, and automating the backward pass.

13
00:00:55,767 --> 00:00:59,167
Last, adding up gradients, and training a network.

14
00:00:59,767 --> 00:01:02,567
It all starts with a question about slopes.

